title: Pelvic lymph node anatomy for prostate cancer staging
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Key distinction:'' In [[Prostate cancer]], regional nodal metastases are ''N1''; nonregional nodal metastases are ''M1a''. Classify the nodal station, not simply whether it lies below the aortic bifurcation.
* ''Regional (green):'' True-pelvic nodes below the division of each common iliac artery into internal and external iliac arteries: internal iliac (hypogastric), external iliac, obturator, periprostatic, and sacral (including presacral) nodes. Hypogastric and internal iliac name the same basin.
* ''Distant (red):'' Common iliac, aortic/para-aortic, other retroperitoneal, inguinal/femoral, and supraclavicular nodes. Inguinal nodes remain nonregional despite their inferior location.
* ''Board pearl:'' External iliac involvement → N1; common iliac involvement → M1a. “Pelvic adenopathy” is insufficiently specific: identify the actual nodal chain.
* ''MRI assessment:'' Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) helps detect nodes; T1-weighted images help assess borders and signal characteristics. Suspicious features in the supplied radiology reference are round nodes with short axis ≥8 mm, oval nodes ≥10 mm, heterogeneous signal, or irregular margins.
* ''Caveat:'' These are suspicion criteria, not proof of metastasis. Size alone poorly separates benign from malignant nodes; small nodes can harbor tumor. Anatomic location determines the staging category once involvement is established.

